2. Covered deployments

A "Live Deployment" is use of the Software for operational workloads on an
Internet Computer subnet or an equivalent execution environment, including
private, permissioned, enterprise, self-hosted and forked environments. Internal
enterprise workloads count even if no third party pays for them. Isolated
development replicas and simulators used solely for non-operational development
or testing are excluded. Calling an operational deployment a test is not an
exemption.

A "Cloud Engine" is a Live Deployment on a subnet or execution environment
offered or operated as a Cloud Engine, including through Open Cloud, or an
equivalent private, sovereign, dedicated or permissioned environment running
the Internet Computer Protocol or a fork or adaptation of it, whose capacity
or node set is reserved for, selected by or controlled by a customer, enterprise
or affiliated group rather than operated as an ordinary shared public subnet.
Branding, ownership, provider, payment currency and connection to mainnet do not
change this classification. Private canister data alone does not make an
ordinary shared public subnet a Cloud Engine.

3. Required mainnet ICP burning

Except for the express organization exception in section 5, your permission
for a Live Deployment is conditional on the following requirements.

For a non-Cloud-Engine deployment, 100% of the ICP used to fund its protocol
resource charges must be irreversibly burned on the canonical Internet Computer
mainnet through the ICP-to-cycles mechanism. All resource credits consumed by
the deployment must be backed by that funding, whether acquired directly or
through a provider. Unbacked, locally minted or simulated credits do not qualify.
This requirement concerns resource funding, not application revenue or user
assets, and does not require a second burn of already qualifying funding.

For a Cloud Engine, ICP with a value of at least 20% of its Infrastructure Base
must be irreversibly burned on that mainnet. "Infrastructure Base" means the
gross infrastructure consideration for the engine capacity hosting the Software,
including reserved capacity, node rental, compute, storage, bandwidth and
in-kind consideration, excluding separately itemized statutory taxes. Do not
deduct provider costs, profits, commissions or affiliate payments. It is not
20% of application revenue or of the token supply.

Free, discounted, internally provided and affiliated infrastructure must not
reduce that base below the independently advertised price of comparable
capacity from an unrelated provider for the same period. Publish the selected
tariff and capacity comparison. If no independently verifiable comparable tariff
exists, obtain a written valuation schedule from Toko Studios Ltd before use;
absence of a price or invoice is not a zero-burn exemption.

For consideration in another currency, publish the conversion to XDR and ICP
using the mainnet protocol's applicable conversion rate at settlement and an
independently published exchange rate where necessary. No private exchange
rate, substitute token, wrapped-token burn or simulated-network burn qualifies.
Actual mainnet ICP burns through protocol conversion may satisfy the threshold;
cycle destruction by itself does not prove an ICP burn. Ordinary transfers to
an account, staking, locking tokens and promises of future burning do not qualify.

Before Cloud Engine operational use begins, publish the deployment identity,
metering method, applicable valuation and actual mainnet burn evidence covering
the initial prepaid or reserved capacity. Continue burning and recording ICP
before using additional capacity. Reconcile the funding and actual Infrastructure
Base for each UTC calendar month within 30 days after its end. That reconciliation
deadline does not permit operation without advance qualifying burn coverage.
If coverage or timely reconciliation is absent, continued Live Deployment is not
permitted. Closure or transfer does not erase unsettled obligations.

4. Verifiable evidence and avoidance

Maintain a publicly accessible audit trail linking each Live Deployment and
accounting period to the resource funding or Infrastructure Base, conversion
calculations and canonical mainnet ICP ledger burn transactions. Include stable
deployment identifiers, transaction/block references, burned amounts and the
allocation of each amount to the covered capacity and period. Preserve records
for at least three years after the applicable period. Application contents and
personal data need not be disclosed, but redaction must not prevent verification
of the required amounts, deployment association or accounting completeness.

Provider or pooled funding is permitted only with independently verifiable
allocation and reconciliation. One burn may cover multiple deployments only
when its allocated amounts do not exceed the actual burn. No amount may be
counted twice. A provider statement, invoice, dashboard assertion or local
cycle-burn counter without the required mainnet evidence is insufficient.

You must not use the Software, including host tools and relay services running
elsewhere, to knowingly provision, administer, operate or serve a noncompliant
Live Deployment. Contractors, affiliates, intermediaries, wrappers, proxies,
renaming and modified copies do not remove these conditions. A covered
deployment that fails either the required ICP burning or the evidence obligation
has no permission under this license, subject only to the stated reconciliation
deadline. No automatic conversion to an unrestricted license occurs over time.

